{
  "dom_id": "step-q131529",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<-2.21).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/597125714057269b7adf9c0a2a9bbdde9631cf37bc80518281ab4444c3189717.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>a)=P(Z<-a)=Φ(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z<-2.21).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/bafe03ed398bfbf95124eb389918759ca80c17eb37f32c2eacf98b48df4b05bc.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z>2.21).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/57eec4175c2f6d8b11db24548f973f9ab4a5b980a9e204352655b40cfd4e431f.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-2.21) & =P(Z>2.21) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.21) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.21) \\\\  & =1-0.9864 \\\\  & =0.0136.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
